.TH EWW-GUARD 1 "2026-08-16" "1.0" "sway dotfiles"
.SH NAME
eww-guard \- keep eww to ONE daemon: client guard, rogue-aware watchdog, single resume owner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eww
.I <any client command>
(transparently guarded \[em]
.B ~/.cargo/bin/eww
is a symlink to
.BR ~/.scripts/eww )
.br
.B eww-watchdog.sh
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
On this machine the eww daemon is owned by
.BR eww.service .
eww 0.6.0's own client, however, auto-forks a
.B new
daemon whenever it cannot get a reply from the running one \[em] and before
forking it deletes the IPC socket file. The result is a
.IR "rogue duplicate" :
it steals the socket path, the real daemon keeps its windows but nobody can
reach it any more, and every popup the orphaned daemon had open is stuck on
screen for good. Symptom the operator sees: a widget (the Wi-Fi popup, the
bar in older incidents) that no click, no
.B eww close
and no
.B eww-watchdog
can remove, while
.B eww active-windows
swears nothing is open.
.PP
When does the client give up? Two ways, both measured on 2026-08-15:
.IP \[bu] 2
.BR "stall" :
5 pings, each with a 100 ms read timeout, 200 ms apart (\[ti]1.5 s total). If
the daemon's GTK main thread does not answer any of them (right after resume,
or with its pages parked in zram under memory pressure) \[->] fork. Seen at
19:25 after a 3 h suspend: two concurrent
.I open-windows.sh
runners, the daemon stalled \[>=]2.5 s, and
.B "eww open activate-linux"
forked rogue #1.
.IP \[bu] 2
.BR "slow open" :
the ping succeeds, the
.B open
is delivered and executed, but the reply arrives after the client's 100 ms
timeout \[->] the client reports "Failed to connect" and forks anyway. Now
.B two
daemons show the same popup at the same position; the newest owns the socket,
so only its copy can be closed. Seen at 23:41 and 23:43 with
.BR wifi-popup :
the deflisten child started in the old daemon in the same second the new daemon
was born.
.PP
eww-guard closes the door at three layers:
.IP 1. 3
.B Prevention.
Every eww CLI call runs through
.I ~/.scripts/eww
which injects
.BR \-\-no\-daemonize .
With that flag the fork branch is skipped and a slow/stalled daemon yields a plain
"Failed to connect to daemon" (exit 1) instead of a rogue.
.IP 2. 3
.B Detection + heal.
.I eww-watchdog.sh
runs every minute and restarts
.B eww.service
if any process other than the unit's MainPID listens on the eww socket path
(rogue), if the MainPID holds no listener, or if the socket ignores a ping for
3 s (zombie). ExecStartPre of eww.service pkills every
.B eww
daemon, so the restart sweeps rogues too.
.IP 3. 3
.B Single resume owner.
Only
.B eww-resume.service
reopens windows after suspend; the swayidle
.I after-resume
handler no longer does. Two runners raced each other (each pkills the other's
in-flight CLIs) and that race is what stalled the daemon into rogue #1.
.SH FILES
.SS Guard
.TP
.I $HOME/.scripts/eww
The wrapper. Loops over the arguments: if
.B \-\-no\-daemonize
is already present, or the subcommand is
.B daemon
(eww.service ExecStart), it execs the real binary untouched; otherwise it execs
it with
.B \-\-no\-daemonize
prepended. Uses
.B "exec -a \[dq]$0\[dq]"
so argv[0] stays whatever the caller typed
(\fI~/.cargo/bin/eww\fR or \fIeww\fR), which keeps every
.B pkill \-f
pattern in the units and scripts matching.
.TP
.I $HOME/.cargo/bin/eww
Symlink \[->]
.IR $HOME/.scripts/eww .
This is the path hardcoded in eww.service, the eww scripts (EWW=...) and waybar;
the symlink is what makes the guard reach them all with zero call-site edits.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/lib/eww/eww
The real eww 0.6.0 binary (2c6523a, built 2025-05-11, sha256 94d14f22...).
Kept
.B named eww
so the kernel's comm field of every daemon stays "eww" and
.B "pkill \-x eww"
in eww.service ExecStartPre still finds them.
.SS Watchdog
.TP
.I $HOME/.scripts/eww-watchdog.sh
The probe + heal.
.B \-\-check
prints the verdict ("healthy: main pid N is the only listener on ..." or
"UNHEALTHY: rogue eww daemon(s) on ... pid(s) ...") and never restarts \[em]
use it first when a widget looks stuck.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/systemd/user/eww-watchdog.service
Oneshot, ExecStart=the script above.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/systemd/user/eww-watchdog.timer
OnBootSec=1min, OnUnitActiveSec=1min.
.SS Resume
.TP
.I $HOME/.scripts/eww-resume-watch.sh
(eww-resume.service) Listens to logind PrepareForSleep(false) on D-Bus, waits for
.B eww ping
(\[<=]20 s), for active outputs and for a stable width, then runs
.IR open-windows.sh .
The single owner of "reopen the widgets after suspend".
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/sway/scripts/resume-handler.sh
swayidle after-resume hook. Since 2026-08-16 it only restores the keyboard
backlight; the
.I open-windows.sh
call added on 2026-03-08 (three days before eww-resume.service existed) is gone.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/eww/scripts/open-windows.sh
Opens the persistent windows. Its entry ping is now patient (retries up to
30 s, logs how long the daemon took) instead of aborting after one failed
1.5 s ping. Its
.B "pkill -f \[dq]^$EWW (open|close|update)\[dq]"
sweep stays: a rogue daemon keeps the argv of the CLI that forked it
.RB ( "eww open <window>" ),
so the pattern kills rogues \[em] belt and braces for the day the guard is
bypassed.
.SS Feature scripts touched
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/eww/scripts/wifi-toggle-popup.sh
waybar network module on-click. Decides open vs close from
.B "eww active-windows"
(the daemon's truth) instead of the shadow variable
.IR wifi-popup-open ,
which can desync from the screen after a failed or slow open.
.SS Related units
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/systemd/user/eww.service
ExecStartPre pkills every eww daemon (\-x eww and \-f "^~/.cargo/bin/eww "),
ExecStart runs the daemon (through the guard, with its own \-\-no\-daemonize),
ExecStartPost pings then runs open-windows.sh. Unchanged by eww-guard, but it is
the heal that the watchdog triggers.
.SH DIAGNOSIS
.nf
eww-watchdog.sh --check                       # verdict in one line
ss -xlp | grep eww-server_                    # every LISTENING eww socket + owning pid
systemctl --user show -p MainPID --value eww.service
pgrep -a -x eww                               # daemons; a rogue shows argv "eww open <win>"
readlink -f "$(command -v eww)"               # must end in ~/.scripts/eww
readlink /proc/$(pgrep -x eww | head -1)/exe  # must be ~/.local/lib/eww/eww
journalctl -t eww-watchdog                    # heals, with the pid list they swept
journalctl --user -u waybar.service | grep "Initializing eww daemon"   # a rogue being born
.fi
.PP
More than one pid on the same
.I eww-server_<hash>
path, or a MainPID that is not among them, is the disease. Old daemons keep
listening on an orphaned socket inode that no path points to any more, so they
show up in
.B ss
but are unreachable by any client.
.SH TESTING
The guard is proven with an isolated config dir (its own socket path, nothing of
the real setup involved):
.nf
EWW=~/.cargo/bin/eww    # absolute path on purpose: argv[0] must match the pgrep patterns below
D=$(mktemp -d); printf '(defwidget w [] (label :text "x"))\\n(defwindow small :monitor 0 :geometry (geometry :x "10px" :y "10px" :anchor "bottom left") (w))\\n' > $D/eww.yuck; : > $D/eww.scss
setsid $EWW -c $D daemon --no-daemonize >$D/log 2>&1 </dev/null &
until $EWW -c $D ping >/dev/null 2>&1; do sleep 0.1; done
d1=$(pgrep -f "^$EWW -c $D daemon"); kill -STOP $d1; (sleep 2; kill -CONT $d1) &
$EWW -c $D open small >$D/cli 2>&1 </dev/null; echo "exit=$?"; sleep 1.5
pgrep -fc "^$EWW -c $D"          # MUST print 1 (guarded). Unguarded eww prints 2.
pkill -CONT -f "^$EWW -c $D"; pkill -f "^$EWW -c $D"
.fi
Measured 2026-08-16: unguarded 2/2 runs forked a second daemon (exit 0, "Initializing
eww server"); guarded 2/2 runs stayed at one daemon (exit 1, "Failed to connect to daemon").
Do not capture the CLI's stdout with $(...) in that test: an unguarded fork inherits the
pipe and the substitution hangs forever.
.SH GOTCHAS
.IP \[bu] 2
.B "cargo install eww"
(or any reinstall) overwrites the symlink at
.I ~/.cargo/bin/eww
with a real binary and silently disables the guard. Move the new binary to
.I ~/.local/lib/eww/eww
and recreate the symlink. If you forget, the watchdog will start logging
"rogue eww daemon(s)" heals \[em] that log line is your reminder.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B EWW_CMD
(the magic variable available inside yuck) expands to
.IR /proc/self/exe ,
i.e. the real binary, so a future
.B "${EWW_CMD} open ..."
in yuck would bypass the guard. Today no yuck uses EWW_CMD with open (3 uses,
all close/update). Prefer the scripts, or add \-\-no\-daemonize by hand there.
.IP \[bu] 2
A guarded
.B "eww open"
can now exit 1 while the window DID open (the slow-open race). Scripts must not
treat that exit code as "nothing happened" \[em] check
.B "eww active-windows"
if it matters (wifi-toggle-popup.sh does).
.IP \[bu] 2
The old watchdog only pinged the socket. A rogue answers pings perfectly, so it
reported green for 4 h 20 min while the real daemon sat orphaned. "Does the
socket answer" and "is the daemon I manage the one answering" are different
questions; the new probe asks the second one.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B "eww active-windows"
talks to whoever owns the socket path. During an incident it lists the newest
rogue's windows only \[em] an empty list is not "nothing is on screen".
.IP \[bu] 2
Rogue daemons forked from waybar's on-click inherit waybar's journal: their
banner ("Initializing eww daemon") and errors show up as
.BR waybar[<pid>] ,
not under eww.service. Rogues forked from a sway session log nowhere.
.IP \[bu] 2
Memory pressure is the amplifier: with \[ti]11 GB parked in zram (swappiness 150)
an idle daemon must fault its pages back in before it can answer, and 100 ms is
not much. The guard makes that a failed click instead of a rogue; it does not
make the daemon faster.
.SH NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
Reload after editing the units:
.B "systemctl --user daemon-reload"
(the timer keeps ticking). The wrapper and the scripts need no reload.
.IP \[bu] 2
Heal by hand when in doubt:
.B "systemctl --user restart eww.service"
\[em] ExecStartPre sweeps every daemon, rogues included; ExecStartPost reopens the
persistent windows.
.IP \[bu] 2
Upstream: eww crates/eww/src/main.rs, branch
.B "Err(err) if action.can_start_daemon() && !opts.no_daemonize"
(remove socket file, fork server). client.rs sets a 100 ms read timeout;
ipc_server.rs waits 100 ms for the app's answer before closing the stream \[em]
the two timers start microseconds apart, so the client can lose the race under load.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
